<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonio Beccadelli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1394">1394</a>–<a href="/years/1471">1471</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Panormita, Il Panormita</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonio Beccadelli (1394–1471), called Panormita after his native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, was an Italian humanist and poet. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> epigrams, the Hermaphroditus (1425), made him famous and notorious. From the 1430s he served Alfonso V of Aragon as secretary and court scholar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, where the circle of humanists he gathered grew into the academy later named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pontano">Giovanni Pontano</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-beccadelli.jpg" alt="Antonio Beccadelli" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giuseppe Emanuele Ortolani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antonio_Beccadelli,_Panormita.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
